From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: read the patch readback from the MCU address itself

041 sampled the download at bus offset 0x090000, i.e. MCU address 0x00090000,
because it assumed the bus offset is the MCU address shifted right by four.
That assumption is wrong.  The driver reaches the MCU space 1:1 through the
BAR and the hardware only window-decodes the upper address bits:
CONN_HOST_CSR_TOP_BASE is 0xE0000 with the comment "// 0x18060000", and
mt7993_fwmode_conf() reads SWDEF_MODE at 0x009143C without any shift, which
lands on 0x1849_143C.

So the eight words 041 printed came from an unrelated SRAM block, and its
conclusion - that the payload never reached 0x00900000 - does not hold.  Read
the payload back at the address the ROM was told to use, and keep the old
address as a control so the two are distinguishable in one log.

Also expose PATCH_FINISH_REQ's check_crc flag as diag_crc_check.  With it set
the ROM verifies the downloaded image against the CRC in the patch header
before jumping, so a rejection proves the transfer is broken while a jump
into the image proves the transfer worked and the image itself is at fault.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -229,6 +229,15 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * PATCH_FINISH_REQ carries a check_crc flag.  With it set the ROM verifies
+ * what it received against the CRC in the patch header, so a rejection
+ * proves the download is broken rather than the image being wrong.
+ */
+static bool diag_crc_check;
+module_param(diag_crc_check, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_crc_check, "let the ROM verify the patch CRC");
+
 static int
 mcu_wm_start_patch(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev)
 {
@@ -248,6 +257,8 @@
 		.dest = MCU_DEST_WM,
 		.resp = NULL,
 	};
+
+	req.check_crc = diag_crc_check ? 1 : 0;
 
 	return mtk_mcu_tx_nocheck(dev, &mcu_txblk);
 }
@@ -405,17 +416,33 @@
 		{
 			int k;
 
-			u32 base = addr >> 4;
-
+			/*
+			 * The bus offset is the MCU address itself: the BAR only
+			 * window-decodes the upper address bits (MCU 0x009143C is
+			 * reached as bus 0x009143C and lands on 0x1849_143C), it
+			 * never shifts the address down.  041 divided by 16 and
+			 * sampled MCU 0x00090000, a different SRAM block, which is
+			 * why the payload looked absent.
+			 */
 			for (k = 0; k < 8; k++) {
 				u32 off = k * 4;
 
 				dev_err(dev->dev,
-					"diag sram 0x%06x (=0x%08x>>4) +0x%02x: rd=0x%08x exp=0x%08x\n",
-					base + off, addr, off,
-					bus_read(dev->bus_trans, base + off),
+					"diag sram 0x%08x +0x%02x: rd=0x%08x exp=0x%08x\n",
+					addr, off,
+					bus_read(dev->bus_trans, addr + off),
 					(off + 4 <= len) ?
 						mcu_diag_word(dl + off) : 0);
+			}
+
+			/* keep the 041 address as a control: it must not match */
+			for (k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
+				u32 off = k * 4;
+
+				dev_err(dev->dev,
+					"diag ctrl 0x%06x +0x%02x: rd=0x%08x\n",
+					(addr >> 4) + off, off,
+					bus_read(dev->bus_trans, (addr >> 4) + off));
 			}
 		}
 	}
